<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:bal="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/bal">
  <!-- A Burn bootstrapper around the MSI (pdfcraft.wxs): it produces the .exe setup file a
       Windows user double-clicks. The MSI keeps doing the real work; the bundle is the
       front door. The MSI is hidden inside the bundle (Visible="no"), so Programs and
       Features lists one "PdfCraft" entry, and the bundle owns the upgrade identity
       (the MSI keeps its own UpgradeCode, MajorUpgrade and per-user/machine scope
       untouched).
       "Learning Machines LLC": the same publisher as the MSI.
       The internal UI bootstrapper application drives the MSI's own dialogs, so the setup
       .exe behaves exactly like the MSI: same welcome page, same install-folder picker,
       same shortcut and right-click-menu checkboxes, same per-user/per-machine choice.
       The UpgradeCode below is new, distinct from the MSI's, on purpose: the bundle is
       the identity a user upgrades, not the MSI.
       Building this requires the WixToolset.BootstrapperApplications.wixext extension
       (wix extension add -g "WixToolset.BootstrapperApplications.wixext/$version"), which
       install-wix does not add: the default BA does not exist. -->
  <Bundle Name="PdfCraft" Version="$(var.BundleVersion)" Manufacturer="Learning Machines LLC"
          UpgradeCode="5538F61F-93FD-41AA-B334-BF7DEA859629" IconSourceFile="$(var.IconPath)">
    <BootstrapperApplication>
      <bal:WixInternalUIBootstrapperApplication />
    </BootstrapperApplication>
    <Chain>
      <MsiPackage Id="PdfCraft" SourceFile="$(var.MsiPath)" Visible="no" DisplayName="PdfCraft" />
    </Chain>
  </Bundle>
</Wix>
